00:57:41.690 --> 00:57:44.000
Same thing with Christy
in terms of her reading

00:57:44.000 --> 00:57:45.230
those type of books.
Right.

00:57:45.230 --> 00:57:48.570
Either parenting or self-improvement

00:57:48.570 --> 00:57:50.854
or relationship improvement.
It might just be

00:57:50.854 --> 00:57:52.730
that we're perfect and we don't need it

00:57:52.730 --> 00:57:55.803
but I wasn't gonna be the
first one to say that.

00:57:55.803 --> 00:57:59.281
She's reading a book
called Mating in Captivity.

00:57:59.281 --> 00:58:01.020
Which I thought okay.

00:58:01.020 --> 00:58:04.170
What?
All right, handcuffs.

00:58:04.170 --> 00:58:05.220
What is this about?

00:58:06.290 --> 00:58:10.399
It's Esther Perel who has written,

00:58:10.399 --> 00:58:14.330
she's a relationships expert
and she's written this book

00:58:14.330 --> 00:58:15.840
that's super popular at this point

00:58:15.840 --> 00:58:20.100
and Jessie's reading it
and based on this question,

00:58:20.100 --> 00:58:22.510
it's all about and again,

00:58:22.510 --> 00:58:26.390
I am speaking from total
second-hand knowledge of the book,

00:58:26.390 --> 00:58:28.450
having just been next
to someone in a hammock

00:58:28.450 --> 00:58:29.940
who is reading it.

00:58:29.940 --> 00:58:31.580
Okay literally second-hand.

00:58:31.580 --> 00:58:33.800
That's the kind of source that I am here.

00:58:33.800 --> 00:58:36.283
But Jessie would stop and
say something and like,

00:58:37.310 --> 00:58:39.680
it led to conversations
about our relationship

00:58:39.680 --> 00:58:43.380
because it's all about
this interesting paradox

00:58:43.380 --> 00:58:45.930
that exists in long-term
relationships and that is,

00:58:47.562 --> 00:58:51.770
the fact that sort of the
separateness and the distance

00:58:51.770 --> 00:58:55.430
that you have with someone
that you are just meeting,

00:58:55.430 --> 00:58:57.173
just getting to know,

00:58:59.030 --> 00:59:04.030
drives the desire and
the romantic connection

00:59:04.730 --> 00:59:07.020
between two people,
it's that unfamiliarity

00:59:07.020 --> 00:59:08.080
that you have with somebody

00:59:08.080 --> 00:59:09.650
that you're kinda getting
to know and falling

00:59:09.650 --> 00:59:12.053
in love with that draws you closer.

00:59:13.260 --> 00:59:17.270
But then, so love kind
of closes the distance

00:59:17.270 --> 00:59:20.960
between two people but
it's the distance and sort

00:59:20.960 --> 00:59:25.000
of the separateness and the
tension that fuels desire.

00:59:25.000 --> 00:59:27.070
And so what happens in
a lot of relationships

00:59:27.070 --> 00:59:31.144
over the long-term is,
there's so much familiarity

00:59:31.144 --> 00:59:34.530
that there's no more mystery
and then people start thinking

00:59:34.530 --> 00:59:36.696
well I'm not even
attracted to this person.

00:59:36.696 --> 00:59:41.490
There's no desire here, it's
just this love relationship

00:59:41.490 --> 00:59:44.690
and she in this very exquisite
way kind of breaks down this

00:59:44.690 --> 00:59:46.520
from a psychological perspective.

00:59:46.520 --> 00:59:48.720
I'm definitely gonna read
the book at this point

00:59:48.720 --> 00:59:50.620
based on just the few things
that Jessie has told me

00:59:50.620 --> 00:59:53.563
but we were kinda talking about that,

00:59:55.723 --> 00:59:57.639
and I think that,

00:59:57.639 --> 00:59:58.940
I would say

00:59:59.919 --> 01:00:02.460
that in relationships, first of all,

01:00:02.460 --> 01:00:06.353
relationships can last
for, it can be indefinite

01:00:06.353 --> 01:00:08.560
if people are just committed to each other

01:00:08.560 --> 01:00:10.780
or just committed to the idea
of staying with each other

01:00:10.780 --> 01:00:12.772
and there's lots of people
who just stay with each other

01:00:12.772 --> 01:00:16.471
for external reasons,
for the sake of children,

01:00:16.471 --> 01:00:18.740
because they believe
that divorce is wrong,

01:00:18.740 --> 01:00:20.840
whatever the reason is,
they just stay together

01:00:20.840 --> 01:00:23.350
because of some expectation
but it's not actual desire

01:00:23.350 --> 01:00:24.850
to stay together right?

01:00:24.850 --> 01:00:28.240
So I would say that in
relationships that are long-term,

01:00:28.240 --> 01:00:31.660
like we're saying over the
course of several years or more

01:00:31.660 --> 01:00:33.860
or whatever, 10 years
or more, I don't know.

01:00:35.040 --> 01:00:38.060
I think that one of the
keys to people continuing

01:00:38.060 --> 01:00:41.540
to be together in a healthy
sort of vibrant situation

01:00:42.830 --> 01:00:45.030
is something that they've
done, either intentional

01:00:45.030 --> 01:00:47.160
or unintentional, we can talk about that,

01:00:47.160 --> 01:00:52.160
has led to there being
enough of a separation

01:00:54.380 --> 01:00:57.400
and distance and mystery that
has maintained some level

01:00:57.400 --> 01:01:00.360
of desire and of course
that's gonna ebb and flow

01:01:00.360 --> 01:01:02.060
over the course of a relationship.

01:01:03.070 --> 01:01:06.210
But it's a fascinating
concept because I think

01:01:06.210 --> 01:01:08.930
that people all the time they say like

01:01:08.930 --> 01:01:11.826
you gotta spice things up in
the bedroom man, you know?

01:01:11.826 --> 01:01:14.650
You gotta get Google involved.

01:01:14.650 --> 01:01:17.200
You gotta have Google to
do things automatically,

01:01:17.200 --> 01:01:19.410
because if not, it's gonna
seem like the same old thing

01:01:19.410 --> 01:01:21.973
and this repetition and familiarity,

01:01:22.980 --> 01:01:26.730
if you're a normal human,
eventually you're gonna be like,

01:01:26.730 --> 01:01:27.740
where's the mystery?

01:01:27.740 --> 01:01:29.430
What is there left to talk about?

01:01:29.430 --> 01:01:32.010
There's a Jason Isbell song
that talks about the old couple

01:01:32.010 --> 01:01:35.980
sitting across from each
other in the restaurant

01:01:35.980 --> 01:01:38.950
and they basically got nothing
else to say to each other.

01:01:38.950 --> 01:01:40.910
Just got nothing else
to learn about his heart

01:01:40.910 --> 01:01:42.660
or something like that is the line.

01:01:43.665 --> 01:01:47.320
And it says, then it's like,
baby, let's never get that way

01:01:47.320 --> 01:01:49.230
is the words to that song and I think

01:01:49.230 --> 01:01:52.290
that it's having desire
to never get that way

01:01:52.290 --> 01:01:54.140
where you got nothing left to talk about.

01:01:54.140 --> 01:01:56.350
Now, I don't have anything,
any prescriptive thing

01:01:56.350 --> 01:01:58.640
about it but what is
your initial assessment

01:01:58.640 --> 01:02:00.573
of that dynamic?

01:02:01.500 --> 01:02:03.360
Well first of all the title of the book

01:02:03.360 --> 01:02:08.320
Mating in Captivity, so
the captivity is marriage?

01:02:08.320 --> 01:02:10.320
So it seems like--
I guess so.

01:02:10.320 --> 01:02:14.170
It's framed in a negative
way which I find interesting

01:02:14.170 --> 01:02:17.365
that then, it's like you're trapped

01:02:17.365 --> 01:02:20.180
in a committed relationship,

01:02:20.180 --> 01:02:22.960
but obviously that's
not the thrust of the--

01:02:22.960 --> 01:02:24.110
No no no.
Of the book.

01:02:24.110 --> 01:02:29.110
It's basically, as far as I understand it,

01:02:29.530 --> 01:02:34.530
it's about finding that
kind of passion and desire

01:02:34.670 --> 01:02:36.860
and knowing also that,

01:02:38.950 --> 01:02:42.020
almost being familiar with
how the human mind works

01:02:42.020 --> 01:02:42.930
and how--
It helps.

01:02:42.930 --> 01:02:46.365
Understanding it isn't that
people, people are like,

01:02:46.365 --> 01:02:47.998
I was so in love at one point

01:02:47.998 --> 01:02:48.831
and now I just don't love you anymore

01:02:48.831 --> 01:02:50.320
and I want a new romance.

01:02:50.320 --> 01:02:52.420
This happens again and
again and again and again.

01:02:52.420 --> 01:02:54.070
I don't feel infatuated or--
She actually talked about

01:02:54.070 --> 01:02:56.940
the hormones, the hormones
that are predominant

01:02:56.940 --> 01:03:01.310
in that falling in love,
initial romance sort of spark,

01:03:01.310 --> 01:03:04.450
those last for a certain
time and then they wane,

01:03:04.450 --> 01:03:06.910
but the hormones that
actually contribute to things

01:03:06.910 --> 01:03:11.910
like cuddling, they last a
lot longer and last way into,

01:03:12.180 --> 01:03:14.370
deep into a relationship.
Well it's interesting

01:03:14.370 --> 01:03:16.840
because Christy and I
were having a discussion

01:03:16.840 --> 01:03:20.060
when we were sleepin' baggin' it up,

01:03:20.060 --> 01:03:23.903
enjoying the dolphins
and the mesmerizing ocean

01:03:23.903 --> 01:03:25.503
and the Instagrammers.

01:03:27.910 --> 01:03:29.520
I don't remember what
got me talking about this

01:03:29.520 --> 01:03:31.940
but it is related to your point.

01:03:31.940 --> 01:03:33.293
And that is,

01:03:34.940 --> 01:03:39.827
I said, "Have you ever,

01:03:39.827 --> 01:03:41.857
"do you ever just have
this overwhelming sense

01:03:41.857 --> 01:03:43.187
"of being alone?"

01:03:44.620 --> 01:03:49.493
Because I rarely have that sense.

01:03:50.560 --> 01:03:53.570
I've always had, I've never lived alone.

01:03:53.570 --> 01:03:56.290
I've always had a roommate
and then I moved from having

01:03:56.290 --> 01:03:58.430
a college roommate, you
and our other friends,

01:03:58.430 --> 01:04:03.430
directly into living with her.

01:04:03.940 --> 01:04:06.300
None of us, none of the four
of us have ever lived alone.

01:04:06.300 --> 01:04:11.030
And I was like sometimes I think in more

01:04:12.600 --> 01:04:15.607
the meditative zone of being on the beach

01:04:15.607 --> 01:04:18.840
and having that discipline
that I talked about

01:04:18.840 --> 01:04:21.360
to take in the environment
and appreciate it

01:04:21.360 --> 01:04:24.750
and find entertainment
or whatever, you know,

01:04:24.750 --> 01:04:26.560
find fill in the blank in it

01:04:26.560 --> 01:04:28.583
without trying to be distracted.

01:04:32.179 --> 01:04:33.590
I did have this overwhelming sense of,

01:04:33.590 --> 01:04:35.793
there is an aspect of being,

01:04:37.260 --> 01:04:41.550
there's a certain autonomy
to no one can ever be

01:04:41.550 --> 01:04:44.400
inside your own brain, you
know, no one can ever be

01:04:44.400 --> 01:04:45.870
inside your own consciousness.

01:04:45.870 --> 01:04:48.390
At a certain point when
you boil it all down,

01:04:48.390 --> 01:04:53.390
you are encapsulated and alone, you know?

01:04:53.679 --> 01:04:55.930
You're your own entity.

01:04:55.930 --> 01:04:57.663
As much as I share with Christy.

01:05:00.150 --> 01:05:02.160
So I ask her that 'cause I was like,

01:05:02.160 --> 01:05:05.407
but I rarely feel that
and I actually think

01:05:05.407 --> 01:05:08.830
and I told her, well I ask
her the question first.

01:05:08.830 --> 01:05:11.527
I said, "Do you ever feel
that way?" and she was like,

01:05:11.527 --> 01:05:14.097
"Yeah, a lot.

01:05:14.097 --> 01:05:17.056
"I have a sense of being alone.

01:05:17.056 --> 01:05:21.740
"And sometimes that
translates into depression."

01:05:21.740 --> 01:05:23.500
You're saying like a
sadness of being alone.

01:05:23.500 --> 01:05:26.870
Yeah a sadness of being
alone because, and I think

01:05:26.870 --> 01:05:28.930
and I said--
'Cause sometimes I just go

01:05:28.930 --> 01:05:31.743
to the bathroom and shut the
door and I don't even poop.

01:05:33.110 --> 01:05:34.093
Just to be alone.

01:05:35.170 --> 01:05:39.720
But having an overwhelming
sense of being lonely

01:05:39.720 --> 01:05:44.720
is an aspect of depression,
it can be I guess.

01:05:45.460 --> 01:05:49.340
So she was laughing at me that
I had not experienced that

01:05:49.340 --> 01:05:51.010
and I--
Do you ever feel alone?

01:05:51.010 --> 01:05:55.113
And I talked about it as if
it was such a foreign concept.

01:05:55.970 --> 01:05:58.580
So to many people including her it's not.

01:05:58.580 --> 01:06:00.280
And she was able to laugh in that moment

01:06:00.280 --> 01:06:02.840
which I thought was beautiful.

01:06:02.840 --> 01:06:05.850
And I was like, I think the
reason why I don't is because

01:06:05.850 --> 01:06:08.080
I run from that feeling.

01:06:08.080 --> 01:06:09.980
It's not that it's there but I spent a lot

01:06:09.980 --> 01:06:14.980
of my life not being comfortable

01:06:15.110 --> 01:06:18.163
with the sensation of loneliness.

01:06:20.230 --> 01:06:23.650
And I equate it with being
alone with my own thoughts,

01:06:23.650 --> 01:06:25.560
being alone with myself.

01:06:25.560 --> 01:06:29.270
As connected as Christy and I
are, we're not the same person

01:06:29.270 --> 01:06:31.620
so there is that tension that I think

01:06:31.620 --> 01:06:34.570
what you said made me
think of that because,

01:06:34.570 --> 01:06:36.100
and again, I'll come
back to it one more time,

01:06:36.100 --> 01:06:41.100
I think one of the keys to
Christy and I being together

01:06:43.270 --> 01:06:46.100
for 19 years and anticipating
being together the rest

01:06:46.100 --> 01:06:49.000
of our lives, that's absolutely the plan.

01:06:49.000 --> 01:06:52.170
And having a confidence in that is...

01:06:54.790 --> 01:06:56.850
On a practical level,
we're just so compatible,

01:06:56.850 --> 01:06:59.550
like everything that
I said about this trip

01:06:59.550 --> 01:07:02.511
like making my list and
the things that I valued

01:07:02.511 --> 01:07:07.511
and being in comfy clothes
and the fact that I wanted

01:07:07.740 --> 01:07:12.740
to design our anniversary
vacation in the way that I did

01:07:13.300 --> 01:07:16.140
and it seemed so perfect to me and Christy

01:07:16.140 --> 01:07:18.730
thought it was funny, the
things that I was doing

01:07:18.730 --> 01:07:21.440
in terms of puttin' chips and cozy pants

01:07:21.440 --> 01:07:23.330
on the list but at the same time,

01:07:23.330 --> 01:07:25.803
I knew that she was also really into it.

01:07:27.820 --> 01:07:31.200
And that's just a case
study in how I think

01:07:31.200 --> 01:07:32.860
we're very compatible when it comes

01:07:32.860 --> 01:07:34.410
to the way we think and our tastes

01:07:34.410 --> 01:07:38.943
and the way that our brains
work, the things that we value.

01:07:39.830 --> 01:07:43.670
At the same time, we have
changed so much as people

01:07:43.670 --> 01:07:46.500
from who we were 19 years ago.

01:07:46.500 --> 01:07:47.333
Yeah.

01:07:47.333 --> 01:07:49.270
We're totally different
people and I know that you

01:07:49.270 --> 01:07:51.340
and Jessie, as well as I know you guys,

01:07:51.340 --> 01:07:53.520
in many aspects are
totally different people.

01:07:53.520 --> 01:07:56.462
We've grown so much, we've
encountered so much in life

01:07:56.462 --> 01:07:59.490
and with everything you encounter,

01:07:59.490 --> 01:08:01.580
the big things and the cumulative effect

01:08:01.580 --> 01:08:03.513
of all the small things
that you wouldn't even put

01:08:03.513 --> 01:08:07.810
on a list are all opportunities

01:08:07.810 --> 01:08:11.640
to grow apart or grow together.

01:08:11.640 --> 01:08:14.380
And I think just the
glue of our compatibility

01:08:14.380 --> 01:08:18.740
has allowed us to face change

01:08:20.860 --> 01:08:22.540
and continue to develop

01:08:22.540 --> 01:08:25.990
as people in a parallel path.

01:08:25.990 --> 01:08:30.720
But I also think that
it's not 100% parallel

01:08:30.720 --> 01:08:35.720
and we don't always respond
to things that happened

01:08:36.860 --> 01:08:39.680
in our lives in the same way or think

01:08:39.680 --> 01:08:42.570
or believe the same things
or agree on the same things

01:08:42.570 --> 01:08:44.190
or whatever at every point.

01:08:44.190 --> 01:08:46.580
Well and I would--
So I'm getting at

01:08:46.580 --> 01:08:49.123
there's that autonomy of yeah,

01:08:50.300 --> 01:08:53.195
we are individuals going
through life together--

01:08:53.195 --> 01:08:54.827
And I think that--
And that tension--

01:08:54.827 --> 01:08:59.827
That's key and I wouldn't
have, I mean I did say

01:09:00.020 --> 01:09:02.740
and I think I said it on
Ear Biscuits at some point

01:09:02.740 --> 01:09:05.000
when I was talking about
this exact same thing,

01:09:05.000 --> 01:09:07.560
about the fact that we
had changed so much.

01:09:07.560 --> 01:09:08.750
Right.

01:09:08.750 --> 01:09:11.690
And Jessie and I have changed in parallel

01:09:11.690 --> 01:09:16.220
but in different ways
and at different rates

01:09:16.220 --> 01:09:18.623
and in different ways and different times.

01:09:19.931 --> 01:09:22.960
And that has created
tension at points, right?

01:09:22.960 --> 01:09:25.020
There have been points
at which we were so much

01:09:25.020 --> 01:09:28.977
on a different page that you might,

01:09:28.977 --> 01:09:31.050
and I don't think we ever really started

01:09:31.050 --> 01:09:33.260
to realistically think about
this but there have been times

01:09:33.260 --> 01:09:34.930
in our marriage where we
were on a different page

01:09:34.930 --> 01:09:37.292
and you have those
thoughts, you were like,

01:09:37.292 --> 01:09:42.220
is this the beginning of
us growing apart forever?

01:09:42.220 --> 01:09:43.800
Sometimes you're like is
this what it feels like

01:09:43.800 --> 01:09:46.070
when you start to grow apart?

01:09:46.070 --> 01:09:49.260
Now thankfully each and
every time that has happened,

01:09:49.260 --> 01:09:54.013
it's ended up kind of fueling this,

01:09:54.013 --> 01:09:56.920
once we do get on the same
page or there's some sort

01:09:56.920 --> 01:10:00.730
of reconciliation or
whatever, it's this dynamic

01:10:00.730 --> 01:10:02.510
that she's talking about in the book,

01:10:02.510 --> 01:10:05.160
which is like now the desire is renewed

01:10:05.160 --> 01:10:07.933
and you feel like you're
moving into a new phase.

01:10:09.690 --> 01:10:12.340
I'm not talking about oh you
gotta have a little instability

01:10:12.340 --> 01:10:14.550
in order to maintain long-term stability.

01:10:14.550 --> 01:10:17.870
I am kinda saying that but
not saying that you got

01:10:17.870 --> 01:10:19.650
to have purposeful instability.

01:10:19.650 --> 01:10:22.440
I'm just saying that
it has helped maintain

01:10:22.440 --> 01:10:24.904
a certain freshness and
a sense of adventure

01:10:24.904 --> 01:10:27.130
and the fact that--
Tension.

01:10:27.130 --> 01:10:32.130
We both are really committed to change.

01:10:32.440 --> 01:10:33.810
We kind of embrace change

01:10:33.810 --> 01:10:37.313
and it's sort of like
striving for personal change.

01:10:38.210 --> 01:10:39.610
Some of that has been intentional,

01:10:39.610 --> 01:10:42.000
some of that has just happened to us,

01:10:42.000 --> 01:10:43.500
life circumstance or whatever,

01:10:44.530 --> 01:10:47.280
but I kinda feel like there's
been a number of key points

01:10:47.280 --> 01:10:51.810
where I felt like oh we're
embarking on a new journey

01:10:51.810 --> 01:10:55.010
just as a couple, not
just in our relationship,

01:10:55.010 --> 01:10:57.080
just with what we're doing in life.

01:10:57.080 --> 01:11:00.170
It's the same thing, I
remember a good friend of mine

01:11:01.295 --> 01:11:06.295
talked about his wife getting
a drastically new haircut.

01:11:08.100 --> 01:11:09.210
Uh-huh.

01:11:09.210 --> 01:11:13.483
And the way he was
talking, he was just like,

01:11:13.483 --> 01:11:16.490
it's like a whole new woman.

01:11:16.490 --> 01:11:21.343
And his point was is that
that kind of like oh,

01:11:22.450 --> 01:11:26.040
you can't ignore that
that's a dynamic in the way

01:11:26.040 --> 01:11:28.310
that people think and I guess
some people might need that

01:11:28.310 --> 01:11:31.707
more than others but this
willingness to be like

01:11:31.707 --> 01:11:35.780
I'm gonna change, I'm
changing myself, I'm evolving.

01:11:35.780 --> 01:11:37.690
You're evolving, you're changing.

01:11:37.690 --> 01:11:40.750
But underlying all that
change is this commitment

01:11:40.750 --> 01:11:42.530
to be like well we're
doing it for the betterment

01:11:42.530 --> 01:11:44.820
of ourselves but we're also
doing it for the betterment

01:11:44.820 --> 01:11:47.810
of our relationship long-term,
like there's gotta be a sense

01:11:47.810 --> 01:11:51.540
of commitment because you
can't just rely on this,

01:11:51.540 --> 01:11:53.200
ooh the romance comes and goes

01:11:53.200 --> 01:11:55.447
and that's gotta keep hoping
that it keeps coming back

01:11:55.447 --> 01:11:57.680
but there's not an underlying
commitment to be like no no,

01:11:57.680 --> 01:12:00.480
we're committed to each other,
then it's not gonna work.

01:12:02.040 --> 01:12:06.380
Yeah there's a foundational
commitment to love each other

01:12:06.380 --> 01:12:09.180
which involves lots of times

01:12:09.180 --> 01:12:11.474
putting the other person before yourself.

01:12:11.474 --> 01:12:12.760
Yeah.

01:12:12.760 --> 01:12:16.370
But I think there's also a commitment to,

01:12:16.370 --> 01:12:20.397
continuing to like each other
that matters a whole lot

01:12:20.397 --> 01:12:22.483
and it's a different thing, right?

01:12:23.805 --> 01:12:25.960
And I feel like...

01:12:29.200 --> 01:12:33.026
I did have a mentality
that was like, okay,

01:12:33.026 --> 01:12:36.342
earlier in marriage that was like

01:12:36.342 --> 01:12:38.330
okay we're gonna figure this
out, we're gonna have a plan

01:12:39.360 --> 01:12:42.270
and we're gonna agree on everything.

01:12:42.270 --> 01:12:46.890
There's gonna be security
and everything being aligned.

01:12:46.890 --> 01:12:48.300
We're gonna be like those couples,

01:12:48.300 --> 01:12:51.780
we're just gonna start
looking alike and being alike

01:12:51.780 --> 01:12:53.946
and we're gonna become one person.

01:12:53.946 --> 01:12:55.520
And that happens quite a bit by the way,

01:12:55.520 --> 01:12:58.150
and it happens in what would
otherwise be considered

01:12:58.150 --> 01:13:02.290
and could be considered
happy couples in a sense

01:13:02.290 --> 01:13:04.210
in that they're committed
and they're together.

01:13:04.210 --> 01:13:05.650
Now--
But I don't think

01:13:05.650 --> 01:13:06.483
I've chosen that.

01:13:06.483 --> 01:13:08.500
There maybe not be a lot of
spark to that relationship

01:13:08.500 --> 01:13:10.120
but it's solid and it's committed.

01:13:10.120 --> 01:13:12.700
But I think there was a
shift in the fact that

01:13:15.260 --> 01:13:19.100
when life throws crap at you
or whatever the case may be,

01:13:19.100 --> 01:13:21.500
you're changing for whatever the reasons,

01:13:21.500 --> 01:13:26.500
that embracing that as an
opportunity to work through that

01:13:29.240 --> 01:13:31.940
with another person
that you're committed to

01:13:31.940 --> 01:13:36.460
can be extremely rewarding
but it might take awhile.

01:13:36.460 --> 01:13:41.460
But knowing that I'm not just falling back

01:13:41.500 --> 01:13:46.500
on a commitment to love
but I'm also pressing

01:13:46.520 --> 01:13:48.980
into a commitment to like.

01:13:48.980 --> 01:13:50.827
Yeah, definitely.
Helps a lot I think.

01:13:50.827 --> 01:13:54.790
And I would say that another key,

01:13:54.790 --> 01:13:57.580
this is not a comprehensive,
exhaustive list,

01:13:57.580 --> 01:14:00.403
it's just some observations
but I think that,

01:14:01.860 --> 01:14:03.663
well I know that communication,

01:14:05.470 --> 01:14:08.220
I'm sure there's couples who
don't talk about this nature

01:14:08.220 --> 01:14:11.870
of their relationship or the
quality of their relationship

01:14:11.870 --> 01:14:13.770
and how it can be better
and what are the issues

01:14:13.770 --> 01:14:15.990
and that kinda thing and they may,

01:14:15.990 --> 01:14:20.290
especially I would say that
baby boomers and older might be

01:14:20.290 --> 01:14:23.360
in a relationship where
they haven't talked about

01:14:23.360 --> 01:14:25.880
their relationship in 20
years but they're like

01:14:25.880 --> 01:14:28.620
what else are we gonna
do, what else would I do?

01:14:28.620 --> 01:14:29.630
Who else would I go to?

01:14:29.630 --> 01:14:32.603
This is stable, this gives me what I need.

01:14:35.590 --> 01:14:38.853
But I would think especially
for our generation and younger,

01:14:40.080 --> 01:14:41.710
that's not gonna work.

01:14:41.710 --> 01:14:42.860
You know what I'm saying?

01:14:42.860 --> 01:14:46.530
I think that you've got to be able

01:14:46.530 --> 01:14:48.960
to talk about your relationship.

01:14:48.960 --> 01:14:52.420
Like Jessie's reading
this book and she stops

01:14:52.420 --> 01:14:55.950
and asks me questions and we
have a discussion about it

01:14:55.950 --> 01:14:57.470
and I am gonna read the book

01:14:59.230 --> 01:15:00.530
with an intention that,

01:15:00.530 --> 01:15:02.737
well what does this mean
about our relationship?

01:15:02.737 --> 01:15:05.290
And this goes for anything,
this goes for friendships,

01:15:05.290 --> 01:15:09.390
anything right like you gotta
stop every once in awhile

01:15:09.390 --> 01:15:11.566
and actually talk about it.

01:15:11.566 --> 01:15:15.830
We've been talking lately
about, we know couples

01:15:15.830 --> 01:15:18.680
who know couples who are
young married couples

01:15:20.018 --> 01:15:25.018
and they haven't figured
out their sex lives at all

01:15:27.070 --> 01:15:29.023
because they will not talk about it.

01:15:29.860 --> 01:15:30.693
You know what I'm saying?

01:15:30.693 --> 01:15:35.693
They're so not on the same page
because they haven't broken

01:15:36.080 --> 01:15:39.860
through what I guess for some
people is this awkward barrier

01:15:39.860 --> 01:15:42.096
to be like let's talk openly

01:15:42.096 --> 01:15:43.760
about what it is we're hoping

01:15:43.760 --> 01:15:45.810
to accomplish what you want, what I want.

01:15:45.810 --> 01:15:46.980
Yeah.

01:15:46.980 --> 01:15:50.100
And that goes for every single area

01:15:50.100 --> 01:15:52.763
and I would just say that,
and Jessie and I talked

01:15:52.763 --> 01:15:56.350
about this is one of the
things that has kinda gotten us

01:15:56.350 --> 01:15:59.450
through these transitions
and these changes

01:15:59.450 --> 01:16:02.010
and these ups and downs and
learning all this new stuff

01:16:02.010 --> 01:16:04.450
about ourselves is just the fact that

01:16:05.310 --> 01:16:08.510
even when it's been hard,
we've talked about it.

01:16:08.510 --> 01:16:10.080
When we weren't on the same page,

01:16:10.080 --> 01:16:12.180
we were talking about not
being on the same page.

01:16:12.180 --> 01:16:15.710
We didn't just get on different pages

01:16:15.710 --> 01:16:18.600
and then stop talking to each other.

01:16:18.600 --> 01:16:21.510
There may have been a few
days where we weren't talking

01:16:21.510 --> 01:16:25.463
about it but we would always
get back to all right,

01:16:26.610 --> 01:16:30.160
let's have an open dialogue
about our relationship.

01:16:30.160 --> 01:16:33.560
I just don't see how it
can continue without that.

01:16:33.560 --> 01:16:37.143
And to kinda bring this to a conclusion,

01:16:38.180 --> 01:16:41.683
ultimately I'm just gonna
take a position of being,

01:16:42.560 --> 01:16:47.483
I'm so grateful, having over 19 plus

01:16:48.890 --> 01:16:51.023
then two plus years of dating.

01:16:53.781 --> 01:16:58.390
Everything that Christy and I
have been through individually

01:16:58.390 --> 01:17:00.500
and together, I'm just grateful

01:17:00.500 --> 01:17:04.690
that everything's an
opportunity to bring you closer

01:17:04.690 --> 01:17:09.683
or to send you apart a little bit.

01:17:11.143 --> 01:17:14.760
And sometimes both, one thing can do both

01:17:14.760 --> 01:17:19.760
in either order, you know,
it's such a dynamic thing.

01:17:22.240 --> 01:17:27.240
Every relationship but I can't
think of any more dynamic

01:17:27.620 --> 01:17:32.620
than with a partner, being married.

01:17:34.471 --> 01:17:39.471
I just cannot take credit
for us being together

01:17:39.727 --> 01:17:42.310
with the stuff that we
listed out and the insights

01:17:42.310 --> 01:17:43.830
that we feel like we have--

01:17:43.830 --> 01:17:45.180
A lot of it is just luck.

01:17:45.180 --> 01:17:48.990
Yeah and it's just
luck, fortune, blessing,

01:17:48.990 --> 01:17:52.190
whatever you wanna call
it, I'm extremely grateful

01:17:52.190 --> 01:17:56.360
that we find ourselves,
that I've had the privilege

01:17:56.360 --> 01:18:01.360
to go through life with
someone up to this point

01:18:01.430 --> 01:18:05.510
and know someone so well

01:18:05.510 --> 01:18:10.020
and still be loved and liked.

01:18:10.020 --> 01:18:15.020
It's an amazing feeling to be
looking at the 20 year mark

01:18:15.930 --> 01:18:19.110
and knowing that I've got
something this special

01:18:19.110 --> 01:18:24.110
that I hoped for but knew
there were no guarantees

01:18:26.450 --> 01:18:28.398
that it would happen.

01:18:28.398 --> 01:18:31.563
It's kind of become a thing that,

01:18:32.780 --> 01:18:35.370
especially in Los Angeles,

01:18:35.370 --> 01:18:39.200
when you say you've been
married for 18 or 19 years,

01:18:39.200 --> 01:18:40.090
people are like

01:18:42.030 --> 01:18:44.020
is this a joke?

01:18:44.020 --> 01:18:46.760
You guys been married that long?

01:18:46.760 --> 01:18:49.480
I like to interpret it
as oh you look so young,

01:18:49.480 --> 01:18:51.500
first of all, but then
once I get over that

01:18:51.500 --> 01:18:53.490
as not being the reason then--

01:18:53.490 --> 01:18:56.570
And again, and I completely
agree with everything

01:18:56.570 --> 01:19:01.570
that you said that this is not based on,

01:19:02.650 --> 01:19:07.350
listen, if I knew anything,

01:19:07.350 --> 01:19:09.140
it's that I know a whole lot less

01:19:09.140 --> 01:19:10.653
than I thought I did at 20.

01:19:11.600 --> 01:19:14.890
I know a whole lot less
than I thought I did at 30.

01:19:14.890 --> 01:19:16.290
Yeah.
You know what I'm saying?

01:19:16.290 --> 01:19:17.500
Every year that passes,

01:19:17.500 --> 01:19:21.300
I'm just more aware of how
little that I understand.

01:19:21.300 --> 01:19:23.823
It's kind of intimidating.

01:19:25.680 --> 01:19:27.750
And how much is left to
learn about the world

01:19:27.750 --> 01:19:29.603
and myself and my wife.

01:19:32.070 --> 01:19:33.955
So I'm not saying that
we've done anything,

01:19:33.955 --> 01:19:38.360
I feel like what you said, very privileged

01:19:38.360 --> 01:19:40.370
that it's turned out this way.

01:19:40.370 --> 01:19:41.530
'Cause I'm sure there could have been

01:19:41.530 --> 01:19:46.530
in some parallel universe,
there was an event

01:19:46.930 --> 01:19:50.220
that happened in the
relationship of Rhett and Jessie

01:19:50.220 --> 01:19:52.253
that was too much for them to bear.

01:19:53.250 --> 01:19:55.900
You know what I'm saying and
I've been able to avoid that.

01:19:55.900 --> 01:19:58.860
It's not like there was
that thing I can point to

01:19:58.860 --> 01:20:01.930
that was we almost didn't
hang on but we did.

01:20:01.930 --> 01:20:04.870
No it's like I said,
there's been ups and downs

01:20:04.870 --> 01:20:09.870
but this has been an adventure of growing

01:20:12.200 --> 01:20:16.800
in partnership with somebody that I still,

01:20:16.800 --> 01:20:19.950
when I think about I get
to go away for a weekend,

01:20:19.950 --> 01:20:23.240
I'm like I get to go with
her away for a weekend

01:20:24.090 --> 01:20:26.110
and that's something that I want to do.

01:20:26.110 --> 01:20:29.050
But maybe that Strong
guy will be there too.

01:20:29.050 --> 01:20:31.990
If Mark Strong is there,
it'll be even better.

01:20:31.990 --> 01:20:33.620
Or just a strong guy with dogs.

01:20:33.620 --> 01:20:37.493
World-class storied career.

01:20:38.920 --> 01:20:42.800
Best actor of our generation, Mark Strong.

01:20:42.800 --> 01:20:44.400
He looked happy in his marriage.

01:20:45.540 --> 01:20:48.140
He's probably been, he's 55 years old.

01:20:48.140 --> 01:20:52.610
Sorry Mark but you can go
on IMDb and figure that out.

01:20:52.610 --> 01:20:55.730
He looks like he's been
with his wife for awhile.

01:20:55.730 --> 01:21:00.320
Got some teenage kids, looks very happy.

01:21:00.320 --> 01:21:02.010
I'm sure if Mark Strong wants to come on,

